== Butterbeer

Preferences for butter beer can be represented by the following demand curve:

#mitex(`
P_d = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d
`)

The supply curve for butter beer can be represented by the following equation:

#mitex(`
P = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in bottles.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  From the curves, $Q_d = 200 - 2P$ and $Q_s = 2P - 40$. Equilibrium is $Q^* = 80$ bottles at $P^* = 60$ galleons.
]

== Q1 | Price Elasticity

a) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of demand when the price changes from 10 to 30. \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $-1/4$. $Q$ goes from $180$ to $140$: $\varepsilon = \frac{-40/160}{20/20} = -\frac{1}{4}$.
]

b) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of demand when the price changes from 40 to 60. \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $-1$. $Q$ goes from $120$ to $80$: $\varepsilon = \frac{-40/100}{20/50} = -1$.
]

c) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of demand when the price changes from 70 to 90. \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $-4$. $Q$ goes from $60$ to $20$: $\varepsilon = \frac{-40/40}{20/80} = -4$.
]

d) Is demand elastic, unit elastic, or inelastic in a), b), and c)? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  a) inelastic, b) unit elastic, c) elastic. Each price change is $20$ galleons and each quantity change is $40$ bottles, the same slope throughout, yet demand grows more elastic higher up the line.
]

e) Use the midpoint method to find the elasticity of supply when the price changes from 40 to 60. \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $5/3$, elastic. $Q$ goes from $40$ to $80$: $\varepsilon = \frac{40/60}{20/50} = \frac{5}{3}$.
]

== Q2 | Supply & Demand Shifters

Butter beer has culturally been associated with a good time. This all changed when a high profile wizard went viral slamming the taste. At the same time an ingredient of the drink, butter extract, became incredibly difficult for butter beer makers to acquire. Use a graph to discuss the effect these two events had on the butter beer market.

a) After the viral post, the demand curve: _shifted in, stayed the same, shifted out_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Shifted in: a change in tastes lowers what buyers will pay for every bottle.
]

b) After the butter extract shortage, the supply curve: _shifted in, stayed the same, shifted out_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Shifted in: a scarcer input raises the cost of every bottle.
]

{++Separately, suppose wages rise across the wizarding world.++}

c) {++If butter beer is a normal good, the demand curve:++} _shifted in, stayed the same, shifted out_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Shifted out: buyers of a normal good buy more at every price when income rises.
]

d) {++If butter beer is an inferior good, the demand curve:++} _shifted in, stayed the same, shifted out_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      Shifted in: buyers of an inferior good switch to something better when income rises.

      The graph shows the two events from a) and b): demand in, to $P = 80 - \frac{1}{2} Q$, and supply in, to $P = 30 + \frac{1}{2} Q$ (the curves Q3 gives).
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

#pagebreak()

== Q3 | Comparative Statics

{++After the butter extract shortage alone, the supply curve became:++}

#mitex(`
P = 30 + \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

a) Equilibrium price: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ Equilibrium quantity: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $65$ galleons and $70$ bottles. $100 - \frac{1}{2} Q = 30 + \frac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow Q = 70$, $P = 65$: the price rises and the quantity falls.
]

{++Without using numbers, how did the two events together (the viral post and the butter extract shortage) change the market?++}

b) Prices: _increased, stayed the same, decreased, indeterminate_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Indeterminate: demand in pushes the price down, supply in pushes it up.
]

c) Quantity: _increased, stayed the same, decreased, indeterminate_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  Decreased: both shifts lower the quantity.
]

{++After both events, the demand curve became:++}

#mitex(`
P = 80 - \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

d) Equilibrium price: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ Equilibrium quantity: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      $55$ galleons and $50$ bottles. $80 - \frac{1}{2} Q = 30 + \frac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow Q = 50$, $P = 55$.

      With numbers the price question resolves: the viral post (down $20$) outweighs the butter extract shortage (up $10$), so the price ends below the original $60$. The quantity falls, as the qualitative answer said.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

